``` Introduction

A trailing comma after a bare identifier declares it as `nil` -- shorthand for writing `identifier := nil` in full.
```
rescue_boat,
@puts "rescue_boat: `rescue_boat`"   # rescue_boat: nil


``` Same idiom inside a type body

Declaring a field this way inside a `Type { }` body works exactly the same -- one uninitialized field per instance, defaulting to `nil` until something sets it.
```
Raft {
	captain,
	crew,
}

r := Raft()
@puts "r.captain: `r.captain`"   # r.captain: nil
@puts "r.crew: `r.crew`"         # r.crew: nil


``` A bare annotation self-declares too

`x: Number` with no `=` at all behaves the same way -- it's already self-declaring by virtue of the annotation, so it doesn't need `= nil` tacked on just to actually declare something. A bare tag annotation (`<...>`) works the same way.
```
altitude: Number
@puts "altitude: `altitude`"   # altitude: nil

cargo: <String, Number>
@puts "cargo: `cargo`"   # cargo: nil
